    "title": "Game 2: Wolves-Nuggets in Denver",
    "text": "DENVER – The Timberwolves have had more than a few playoff games over the last five years that have swung wildly.They have played a lot of games (regular season and playoffs) against Denver that fit that description. Only a few of those games featured greater swings on the scoreboard and in emotion than Game 2 of their first-round series tonight.After a horrendous start, the Wolves fought back from a 19-point deficit to take a lead — all before halftime. The second half was much tighter on the scoreboard, and the Wolves came away with a 119-114 victory over the Nuggets.The Wolves had trouble containing Jamal Murray early in the game as he had 23 of his 30 points in the first half. Nikola Jokic picked up the scoring slack for Denver in the second half with 24 points, 15 rebounds and eight assists for the game.Anthony Edwards looked like himself after a choppy Game 1 with 30 points for the Wolves while Julius Randle was stellar with 24. Rudy Gobert, who was fourth in the Defensive Player of the Year voting, had some key possessions against Jokic late.The Wolves took a 115-111 lead on a Donte DiVincenzo's three with 1:05 to play. After Gobert played great individual defense on him down the stretch, Jokic got a dunk to pull within two with 49.6 seconds left. Edwards was called for a walk with 30.6 seconds to play setting a Denver chance to tie or take the lead. After the Wolves almost came up with a steal, Julius Randle fouled Christian Braun with 19.1 seconds left. He hit one free throw and the Wolves led 115-114.The Wolves missed free throws all night and were 17-for-28 as they inbounded the ball on the next possession. Randle went to the line for two and drained them both. Murray missed at the other end, and the Wolves clinched the victory. Denver had won 13 games in a row, counting a 12-game winning streak to close the regular season.Game 3 is Thursday at Target Center.",
